  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}}$ is $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ find the value of $c.$\n\n$c=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{18{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(18+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(c-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{18+0}{c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{c} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 36 & =3c \\\\ c & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
